{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfZYjBzppLPCFqYo8t7Cy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 03 is done and the gate passes: `lake build` succeeds, the sorry count is unchanged, and pytest shows 1,231 passed.\n\nThe new file is `lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/GateMatrix.lean`, imported from the root file. It has no `sorry`. The main theorem `tracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one` depends only on Lean's three standard axioms (`propext`, `Classical.choice`, `Quot.sound`).\n\n**What the definitions say.** They follow Möhring (1990) p. 18 and never mention stacks or pathwidth:\n- a net–gate matrix is a relation between nets and gates;\n- for a gate order, the \"augmented matrix\" fills in each net's row between its leftmost and rightmost gate;\n- two nets conflict if their augmented rows share a column;\n- a track assignment puts no two conflicting nets on the same track;\n- `tracksFor π` is the fewest tracks for a fixed gate order, and `tracks` is the fewest over all orders, which is Möhring's `t(M)`;\n- `netGraph` is Möhring's net adjacency graph (p. 29).\n\n**What is proved.** All three main results assume the matrix has at least one 1:\n- `tracksFor_eq_maxOpenStacks`: for a fixed gate order, the fewest tracks equals the largest column sum of the augmented matrix. This is Möhring's left-edge theorem (p. 31) and the real content of the item. The lower bound is a counting argument at one column. The upper bound colours nets greedily in order of their leftmost gate.\n- `tracks_eq_mospValue`: `t(M)` equals the MOSP value of the same matrix (Linhares & Yanasse 2002, Prop. 2). Nets are the customers and gates are the patterns; nothing is transposed.\n- `tracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one`: `t(M)` equals the pathwidth of the net graph plus one. This goes through `netGraph_eq_mospGraph` and the existing `mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one`.\n\nTwo edge cases are stated as separate theorems: an all-zero matrix with at least one net needs one track, and a matrix with no nets needs none.\n\n**One disagreement with the checker.** On an all-zero matrix, `paper2/complex_check.py` says 0 tracks because it ignores empty rows, while Lean says 1 because every net must get a track. The two agree whenever the matrix has a 1, which is the case the main theorems cover. It's a convention choice: Möhring's nets always touch at least one gate. I recorded it in `paper2/equivalences.md` rather than change either side.\n\nI also updated `equivalences.md` (row 2 of the table, plus a new section for item 03), marked item 03 `[x]` in `iterations.md`, and added the iteration 3 entry to `PROGRESS.md` (now 3/14). Nothing is committed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":693,"cache_read_input_tokens":120369,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":693,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":794,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"64fb36f5-ca69-4136-9e97-8b7c01730962","uuid":"a3295bc2-a77e-4f6c-a808-e39da61aa64a","timestamp":"2026-09-30T11:30:39.368Z","request_id":"req_011CfZYjBSamon8tC9vrgB1T"}
